Setting, inferred from this page and its neighbours. I is a compact interval with endpoints ∂I = {ω0, ω1}. A 'découpage' is a finite subdivision T ⊃ ∂I, with points ω0 = t0 < t1 < … < tn = ω1. Its strata are the points ti and the open intervals I_{ti ti+1}; M0 denotes the vertices and M1 the edges. A 'polygone' 𝔏 (machine-transcribed as Φ) is a set of strata. Omb°(𝔏), its 'ombre' or shadow, is the union of those strata. That union is a constructible subset of the interval, i.e. a finite union of points and open intervals.

The dual 𝔏* is the complementary polygon, as the next page makes explicit with C𝔏 = complement in 𝔏_T. δ𝔏 is the set of 'sommets-bord': vertices where 𝔏 is not locally constant.

The previous page sorted vertices into classes:
- isolated (𝔏_is): the vertex is in 𝔏 but its adjacent edges are not;
- lacunary (𝔏_lac): the vertex is not in 𝔏 but its adjacent edges are;
- evanescent (𝔏_év): the vertex and its adjacent edges are all in 𝔏, so it is removable;
- ∂*.

This page enlarges δ𝔏 to δ̂𝔏 = δ𝔏 ∪ ∂I, so that the endpoints of I are always counted. It introduces 'semi-évanescent' endpoints and gets the disjoint decomposition δ̂𝔏 = 𝔏_is ⊔ 𝔏_lac ⊔ 𝔏_év ⊔ 𝔏_sév ⊔ 𝔏*_sév ⊔ ∂*𝔏. Here:
- 𝔏_sév = ∂I \ (∂I ∩ δ𝔏*): endpoints lying in 𝔏 together with their adjacent edge;
- 𝔏*_sév = ∂I \ (∂I ∩ δ𝔏): endpoints lying outside 𝔏 along with their adjacent edge.

Behaviour under complementation 𝔏 ↦ 𝔏*:
- is and lac are exchanged: 𝔏_is = 𝔏*_lac and 𝔏_lac = 𝔏*_is;
- 𝔏_sév and 𝔏*_sév are exchanged;
- ∂* is invariant: ∂*𝔏 = ∂*𝔏*;
- the evanescent vertices disappear: 𝔏*_év = 𝔏**_év = ∅.

An endpoint s ∈ ∂I has exactly four possible positions relative to 𝔏: (in, edge out), (in, edge in), (out, edge in), (out, edge out). These correspond respectively to is, sév, lac and *sév.

The boxed result says that the complement of the shadow is the shadow of the dual: ∁Omb°(𝔏) = ∁Omb°(Omb°(𝔏)) = Omb°(𝔏*). Consequently Cons(M), the constructible parts of M, is stable under complement.

Finally, the page asserts that Omb° restricted to purified polygons gives a bijection Purif(M) → Cons(M). A purified polygon has no evanescent vertices, i.e. it is the minimal stratification adapted to the set. The proof starts here and continues on the next page: from Σ = Cons^b one recovers 𝔏_1 and 𝔏_is as maximal elements for a specialization order.

Purpose: elementary combinatorics of one-dimensional constructible (tame) sets, encoded by minimal polygons and their behaviour under complementation, as a step in a 'tame topology' style workshop.
